 "evidence": "A",
 "licence_note": null,
 "history": "The entari (the Levant's kunbaz, the Egyptian kuftan) was the robe of every townsman over the shirt and drawers: fitted to the hip, the fronts wrapped over and tied with tapes at the side, a round collarless neck, the sleeves to the wrist with small buttons and loops, the skirts 'under the knees' with slits at the sides from the hip; of striped silk-cotton, Aleppo flowered stuffs, Indian silk-cottons or calico, muslin in summer. 'The common people ... are content with the breeches, the enteri, and the benisch' [RUS][NIE][LAN].",
